/**
 * What backs the key the host signs digests with.
 *
 * This is the ONLY thing on this binding that can put the client on the local
 * silent path, and it deliberately costs the host a line of compiled code:
 * the tier used to be a string inside the constructor's config JSON, which is
 * data and could therefore arrive from a file, a server, or anything else the
 * app happened to pipe in.
 *
 * Nothing in Rust can check the claim. On a phone the host is the only party
 * that knows what holds the key, and it is also the party a compromise would
 * own, so what the shape buys is narrower and worth stating exactly: this is
 * the only route by which a tier can arrive at all. The declaration is made
 * once, in the app's own code, and a host that throws instead of answering
 * lands on [`AgentSignerBacking::RelayOnly`]; a tier written into the
 * constructor's config JSON is not quietly downgraded but refused outright,
 * because a host that believes it configured hardware and is silently
 * relay-only reads as a bug in the owner's app rather than as a mis-wiring
 * here. Declaring hardware unlocks only the *local* silent path; the binding
 * limit is still the one the on-chain guard enforces.
 */

public enum AgentSignerBacking {
    
    /**
     * A hardware module on this device holds the key `signDigest` uses.
     */
    case deviceHardware(kind: AgentHardwareKind
    )
    /**
     * No key this process may drive on its own. Every write escalates to the
     * owner.
     */
    case relayOnly
}


